==Old English==

===Etymology===
From an early form ''haebuc'', from {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*habukaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*kep-||seize}}. Cognate with Old Frisian [[havek]] (West Frisian [[hauk]]), Old Saxon [[hauk]] (Dutch [[havik]]), Old High German [[habuh]] (German [[Habicht]]), Old Norse [[haukr]] (Swedish [[hök]]). The word is related to a Slavic word for a bird of prey (Russian {{l|ru|кобец}}, Polish [[kobuz]]).

